C['pachelbel'] = dict(
  name='Johann Pachelbel', dates='1653–1706', era='Baroque', icon='🔁',
  initial='pachelbel-canon',
  idea='Ground bass and canon',
  kicker='Ground bass and canon',
  desc='Meet Johann Pachelbel, play his complete Canon in D for three violins and bass, and hear how a repeating bass line and a strict canon build one of the best-known pieces ever written.',
  intro='Choose the Canon, then press Play. Isolate the cello to hear the two-bar bass that repeats 28 times, or one violin at a time to follow the canon: each violin plays exactly the same music, two bars apart.',
  portrait=dict(src='pachelbel-hexachordum-1699.webp', alt='Printed page of Baroque keyboard music headed Aria Prima, with a melody and its first variation on two staves',
    cap='<strong>Pachelbel in print: Hexachordum Apollinis (Nuremberg, 1699)</strong>No portrait of Johann Pachelbel is known to survive, so here is his music instead: the “Aria Prima” and its first variation from the keyboard collection he dedicated to the organists Dietrich Buxtehude and Ferdinand Tobias Richter. Like the Canon, every variation keeps the same harmony underneath. Bibliothèque nationale de France (RISM), public domain, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hexachordum_Apollinis,_sex_arias_exhibens,_organo_pneumatico_vel_clavato_cymbalo,_modulandas,_quarum_singulis_suae_sunt,_subjectae_variationes..._-_btv1b108711033_(10_of_52).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Johann Pachelbel (1653–1706) was one of the most respected organists in Germany, a teacher of the Bach family, and a master of music built on patterns. Today he is remembered for a single piece he might hardly have recognized as famous: the Canon in D.',
  bio=[
    'Pachelbel was baptized in Nuremberg on 1 September 1653, the son of a wine merchant. He studied at the university in Altdorf and the gymnasium in Regensburg, but money ran short, and he made his living as an organist instead. In 1673 he went to Vienna as deputy organist at St. Stephen’s Cathedral, where he heard the latest Catholic church music from Italy.',
    'He then worked his way through the great organ posts of central Germany: the court at Eisenach (1677), the Predigerkirche in Erfurt (1678–1690), Stuttgart, and Gotha. In Eisenach he became a friend of the Bach family. He was godfather to Johann Sebastian Bach’s sister and taught J. S. Bach’s eldest brother, Johann Christoph, who in turn taught the young Sebastian. A line runs from Pachelbel’s organ bench to Bach’s.',
    'In 1695 he came home to Nuremberg as organist of St. Sebaldus Church, where he stayed until his death in March 1706. He wrote hundreds of organ chorales and fugues, sacred vocal works, and the keyboard variations of <em>Hexachordum Apollinis</em> (1699).',
    'The Canon was written for three violins and basso continuo, probably for a private occasion, and we do not know when. Its only early source is a manuscript copied long after his death. It was first published in 1919 and became a worldwide hit only after a 1968 recording by Jean-François Paillard’s chamber orchestra. Since then it has been used at countless weddings and borrowed by pop songwriters, because its eight-chord pattern fits almost any melody.'],
  timeline=[('1653','Baptized in Nuremberg on 1 September.'),('1673','Deputy organist at St. Stephen’s Cathedral, Vienna.'),('1677','Court organist in Eisenach, where he befriends the Bach family.'),('1678','Organist of the Predigerkirche in Erfurt for twelve years; teaches Johann Christoph Bach.'),('1695','Returns to Nuremberg as organist of St. Sebaldus Church.'),('1699','Publishes the keyboard variations <em>Hexachordum Apollinis</em>.'),('1706','Dies in Nuremberg in March.'),('1919','The Canon and Gigue in D are first published.'),('1968','Jean-François Paillard’s recording makes the Canon world-famous.')],
  idea_text=['A <strong>ground bass</strong> is a short bass line that repeats over and over while the music above it changes. In the Canon, the cello plays the same eight notes (D, A, B, F♯, G, D, G, A) 28 times.',
    'A <strong>canon</strong> is a round: one voice starts a melody and others enter later with exactly the same notes. Violin II plays what Violin I played two bars earlier, and Violin III follows two bars after that. Because every phrase fits the same eight chords, the three violins always agree.'],
  lab_q='Version A plays a short tune once. Version B plays it over a repeating bass. Does the repeating bass make the tune feel more settled or more restless?',
  listen=['Canon in D: count how many times you hear the cello’s eight-note pattern before the violins become very busy.','Gigue in D (the Canon’s companion): listen for the same three violins chasing each other in a fast dance.','Hexachordum Apollinis, Aria Sebaldina: hear Pachelbel vary one melody over an unchanging harmony on the keyboard.'],
  practice=['Use the Source staff menu to choose the cello. Sing or play its eight notes. Where does the pattern start again?','Play Violin I alone for bars 3–6, then Violin II for bars 5–8. What do you notice?','Write your own four-bar melody that fits the first two bars of the bass. Could it become a canon if a partner started two beats later?'],
  check='The cello repeats an eight-note pattern every two bars. Violin II copies Violin I exactly, two bars later, and Violin III two bars after that. That is why the piece is called a canon.',
  related='<a href="bach.html">Continue to J. S. Bach →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Johann-Pachelbel','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Johann Pachelbel'),
)

C['saint-saens'] = dict(
  name='Camille Saint-Saëns', dates='1835–1921', era='Romantic', icon='🦢',
  initial='saint-saens-the-swan',
  idea='Melody over rippling accompaniment',
  kicker='Melody over rippling accompaniment',
  desc='Meet Camille Saint-Saëns, the child prodigy who became France’s great all-round musician, and play his complete “The Swan” from The Carnival of the Animals in an interactive score.',
  intro='Choose “The Swan”, then press Play. The cello sings one long line while the piano ripples underneath, like water under a gliding swan. Isolate the cello staff to practice the melody, or the piano to hear how its arpeggios never stop moving.',
  portrait=dict(src='saint-saens-boulanger-1884.webp', alt='Engraved portrait of Camille Saint-Saëns in profile with a short beard and receding hair',
    cap='<strong>Camille Saint-Saëns, 1884</strong>Portrait by Gustave Boulanger, engraved by Charles Bellan, made in the years when Saint-Saëns was writing his “Organ” Symphony and <em>The Carnival of the Animals</em>. Gustave Boulanger, public domain, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Sa%C3%ABns_portrait_by_Boulanger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Camille Saint-Saëns (1835–1921) was a child prodigy, a dazzling organist and pianist, a teacher, a scientist at heart, and one of the most versatile composers France has produced. His most-loved melody came from a musical joke he refused to publish.',
  bio=[
    'Saint-Saëns was born in Paris on 9 October 1835 and raised by his mother and great-aunt after his father died. He began piano at two and composed at three. At ten he gave his public debut at the Salle Pleyel with concertos by Mozart and Beethoven, and offered, as an encore, to play any of Beethoven’s 32 sonatas from memory.',
    'He studied organ and composition at the Paris Conservatoire, and from 1858 to 1877 was organist of the Église de la Madeleine, one of the best church posts in Paris. Franz Liszt, after hearing him improvise, called him the greatest organist in the world. As a piano teacher at the École Niedermeyer he taught the young Gabriel Fauré, who became a lifelong friend.',
    'After France’s defeat in 1871, he helped found the Société nationale de musique to promote new French music. His works include five piano concertos, the tone poem <em>Danse macabre</em> (1874), the opera <em>Samson et Dalila</em> (1877) and the Symphony No. 3, the “Organ” Symphony (1886). In 1908 he wrote <em>L’Assassinat du duc de Guise</em>, one of the very first original scores composed for a film.',
    'In 1886 he wrote <em>The Carnival of the Animals</em>, fourteen comic movements for a private Mardi Gras concert, full of musical jokes about lions, tortoises, fossils and even pianists. Worried it would hurt his serious reputation, he banned its publication during his lifetime, with one exception: “The Swan”. He travelled widely, studied astronomy, wrote poetry and essays, and died in Algiers on 16 December 1921. <em>The Carnival</em> was published the following year and has been a favorite ever since.'],
  timeline=[('1835','Born in Paris on 9 October.'),('1846','Public debut at the Salle Pleyel, aged ten.'),('1858','Becomes organist of the Église de la Madeleine, Paris.'),('1861','Teaches piano at the École Niedermeyer; Gabriel Fauré is a pupil.'),('1871','Co-founds the Société nationale de musique.'),('1874','Composes the tone poem <em>Danse macabre</em>.'),('1877','<em>Samson et Dalila</em> premieres in Weimar.'),('1886','Writes the “Organ” Symphony and <em>The Carnival of the Animals</em>.'),('1908','Composes one of the first original film scores.'),('1921','Dies in Algiers on 16 December.'),('1922','<em>The Carnival of the Animals</em> is published.')],
  idea_text=['Much Romantic music sets a <strong>singing melody over a moving accompaniment</strong>. In “The Swan”, the piano plays even, rippling broken chords (arpeggios) in sixteenth notes, while the cello holds long, smooth notes above them.',
    'The two layers move at very different speeds. The fast layer gives a feeling of motion; the slow layer floats on top. That contrast is what makes us “see” a swan gliding calmly over moving water.'],
  lab_q='Version A plays a melody alone. Version B adds a rippling accompaniment underneath. Which one sounds more like something gliding over water?',
  listen=['“The Swan”: listen for the cello’s long phrases and where it breathes between them.','“Aquarium”, also from <em>The Carnival of the Animals</em>: compare another watery accompaniment, this time glittering and mysterious.','<em>Danse macabre</em>: hear a solo violin with a deliberately “wrong” tuned string play the role of Death.'],
  practice=['Isolate the cello. Mark every place the melody takes a breath. How many phrases are there?','Isolate the piano. How many notes does the right hand play for each cello note in bar 3?','Name another animal. What kind of accompaniment would you choose to describe how it moves?'],
  check='The piano’s rippling sixteenth notes keep moving the whole time, while the cello plays long notes slurred together. Fast motion underneath and slow motion on top creates the gliding image.',
  related='<a href="faure.html">Continue to his pupil, Gabriel Fauré →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Camille-Saint-Saens','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Camille Saint-Saëns'),
)

C['massenet'] = dict(
  name='Jules Massenet', dates='1842–1912', era='Romantic', icon='🎻',
  initial='massenet-meditation',
  idea='The operatic interlude',
  kicker='The operatic interlude',
  desc='Meet Jules Massenet, the most popular French opera composer of his time, and play the complete “Méditation” from Thaïs in an interactive score.',
  intro='Choose the “Méditation”, then press Play. Follow the singing line as if it were a voice without words: it rises slowly, reaches a passionate climax, and returns to calm. Slow the tempo to hear how each phrase is shaped.',
  portrait=dict(src='massenet-hanfstaengl-1887.webp', alt='Photograph of Jules Massenet seated in an armchair, wearing a suit and holding a cane, with a handwritten dedication above',
    cap='<strong>Jules Massenet, 1887</strong>A signed studio photograph from Frankfurt, taken three years after <em>Manon</em> made him the most performed opera composer in France. Erwin Hanfstaengl, public domain, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jules_Massenet_1887.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Jules Massenet (1842–1912) wrote more than twenty operas and taught a generation of French composers. His best-known music is not sung at all: a violin solo played while the curtain is down.',
  bio=[
    'Massenet was born on 12 May 1842 near Saint-Étienne, the youngest of a large family, and learned piano from his mother. He entered the Paris Conservatoire at eleven, paid his way by playing percussion in theatre orchestras and piano in cafés, and in 1863 won the Prix de Rome, France’s top prize for young composers.',
    'He became a master of opera. <em>Manon</em> (1884), about a young woman torn between love and luxury, made him famous across Europe, and <em>Werther</em> (1892), after Goethe’s novel, is still performed worldwide. From 1878 to 1896 he was professor of composition at the Conservatoire, where his pupils included Gustave Charpentier, Reynaldo Hahn and George Enescu.',
    '<em>Thaïs</em> premiered at the Paris Opéra in March 1894. It tells of a monk who tries to convert a celebrated courtesan in ancient Egypt. Between two scenes of Act II, while Thaïs reflects on her life, the orchestra plays the “Méditation”: a long solo for violin with harp and strings. It became one of the most-loved encore pieces for violinists.',
    'Massenet worked with great discipline, rising before dawn to compose. His later operas include <em>Le jongleur de Notre-Dame</em> (1902) and <em>Don Quichotte</em> (1910). He died in Paris on 13 August 1912.'],
  timeline=[('1842','Born near Saint-Étienne on 12 May.'),('1853','Enters the Paris Conservatoire.'),('1863','Wins the Prix de Rome.'),('1878','Becomes professor of composition at the Conservatoire.'),('1884','<em>Manon</em> premieres at the Opéra-Comique.'),('1892','<em>Werther</em> premieres in Vienna.'),('1894','<em>Thaïs</em>, with its “Méditation”, premieres at the Paris Opéra.'),('1910','<em>Don Quichotte</em> premieres in Monte Carlo.'),('1912','Dies in Paris on 13 August.')],
  idea_text=['An <strong>intermezzo</strong> or <strong>entr’acte</strong> is orchestral music played between scenes of an opera. With no words, it lets the audience feel what a character is thinking.',
    'In the “Méditation”, the violin acts like a singer. Its melody moves mostly by step, rises to a climax in the middle, and falls back to the opening theme. Listen for the way the music grows louder and higher as Thaïs’s feelings grow stronger.'],
  lab_q='Version A plays a melody once. Version B plays it an octave higher and slower, like a singer reaching a climax. What changes in the mood?',
  listen=['“Méditation” from <em>Thaïs</em>: notice where the melody is highest and loudest. What might the character be feeling there?','<em>Manon</em>, “Adieu, notre petite table”: hear Massenet write a quiet farewell for a singer.','<em>Werther</em>, Prelude: compare how the orchestra sets the mood before anyone sings.'],
  practice=['Find the bar where the melody reaches its highest note. How does the music prepare for it?','Play the first four bars, then the last four. What is the same, and what is different?','Choose a moment from a story you know. Describe the music you would write for the time between two scenes.'],
  check='The opening theme returns at the end after a more passionate middle section. That return (A – B – A) gives the piece its feeling of calm after a storm.',
  related='<a href="faure.html">Compare his fellow Parisian, Gabriel Fauré →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Jules-Massenet','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Jules Massenet'),
)

C['grieg'] = dict(
  name='Edvard Grieg', dates='1843–1907', era='Romantic', icon='🌅',
  initial='grieg-morning-mood',
  idea='Musical nationalism and tone painting',
  kicker='Tone painting and Norwegian nationalism',
  desc='Meet Edvard Grieg, Norway’s national composer, and play “Morning Mood” from Peer Gynt in an interactive score while you learn how music paints a sunrise.',
  intro='Choose “Morning Mood”, then press Play. Listen for a short pentatonic tune passed back and forth, like birdcalls, while the music grows brighter and louder, as if the sun were rising.',
  portrait=dict(src='grieg-c-1870.webp', alt='Oval photograph of the young Edvard Grieg with wavy hair and a moustache',
    cap='<strong>Edvard Grieg, about 1870</strong>Grieg in his late twenties, soon after writing his Piano Concerto. Bergen Public Library (Grieg Archives), no known copyright restrictions, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Edvard_Grieg_portrait)_(3469852735).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Edvard Grieg (1843–1907) gave Norway a musical voice. He drew on the dances and folk songs of his country, and his music for Henrik Ibsen’s play <em>Peer Gynt</em> includes two of the most famous tunes ever written: “Morning Mood” and “In the Hall of the Mountain King”.',
  bio=[
    'Grieg was born in Bergen, on Norway’s west coast, on 15 June 1843. His mother, a pianist, gave him his first lessons. The famous Norwegian violinist Ole Bull heard the fifteen-year-old play and persuaded his parents to send him to the Leipzig Conservatory in Germany.',
    'Back in Scandinavia, he met the young composer Rikard Nordraak, who inspired him to write music that sounded Norwegian rather than German. In 1867 he married his cousin, the soprano Nina Hagerup, who sang many of his songs, and in 1868 he wrote his Piano Concerto in A minor, his breakthrough work.',
    'In 1874 the playwright Henrik Ibsen asked him for music for <em>Peer Gynt</em>, a fantastical play about a boastful young man who wanders the world. It opened in Christiania (now Oslo) in 1876. “Morning Mood” begins Act IV, which takes place at dawn in the Moroccan desert, not in a Norwegian valley, even though most listeners picture fjords. Grieg later arranged the best numbers into two orchestral suites.',
    'He wrote 66 short <em>Lyric Pieces</em> for piano, many songs, and the <em>Holberg Suite</em>. From 1885 he lived at Troldhaugen, a villa near Bergen, and toured Europe as a pianist and conductor despite poor health. He died in Bergen on 4 September 1907, a national hero.'],
  timeline=[('1843','Born in Bergen, Norway, on 15 June.'),('1858','Begins study at the Leipzig Conservatory.'),('1867','Marries the soprano Nina Hagerup; publishes his first <em>Lyric Pieces</em>.'),('1868','Composes the Piano Concerto in A minor.'),('1876','Ibsen’s <em>Peer Gynt</em> opens with Grieg’s music.'),('1884','Writes the <em>Holberg Suite</em>.'),('1885','Moves into his villa, Troldhaugen.'),('1888','Publishes the first <em>Peer Gynt</em> Suite, Op. 46.'),('1907','Dies in Bergen on 4 September.')],
  idea_text=['<strong>Tone painting</strong> means using music to describe a scene. In “Morning Mood”, a short tune built on the five-note pentatonic scale is passed between instruments like birdsong, and the music grows louder and fuller as the light grows.',
    '<strong>Musical nationalism</strong> was a movement in the 1800s in which composers used the folk songs, dances and stories of their own countries. Grieg’s drones (held bass notes), folk-dance rhythms and modal melodies made his music sound unmistakably Norwegian.'],
  lab_q='Version A plays a tune alone. Version B plays the same tune over a drone, a held low note like a folk fiddle or bagpipe. Which version sounds more like folk music?',
  listen=['“Morning Mood”: listen for the tune being handed from one instrument (or hand) to another, and for the moment of greatest brightness.','“In the Hall of the Mountain King”: hear one short tune repeat faster and louder until it explodes.','Piano Concerto in A minor, opening: listen for the dramatic falling figure that starts the piece.'],
  practice=['Find the opening tune. Which five different notes does it use?','Follow the score to the loudest bar. How has the texture changed since the beginning?','Choose a time of day other than sunrise. Describe how you would paint it in music: tempo, register, loudness and instruments.'],
  check='The opening tune uses only the notes of a pentatonic scale, which gives it a simple, folk-like sound. The piece grows by adding instruments, register and volume, like a sky filling with light.',
  related='<a href="tchaikovsky.html">Compare another master of musical scenes, Tchaikovsky →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Edvard-Grieg','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Edvard Grieg'),
)

C['faure'] = dict(
  name='Gabriel Fauré', dates='1845–1924', era='Romantic', icon='🕊️',
  initial='faure-pavane',
  idea='Modal harmony and the revived dance',
  kicker='Modal color and the old court dance',
  desc='Meet Gabriel Fauré, the French master of song and subtle harmony, and play his Pavane, Op. 50 in an interactive score.',
  intro='Choose the Pavane, then press Play. Listen for the slow, steady walking pulse of an old court dance under a long, sad melody, and for the unexpected chords Fauré slips in where you expect ordinary ones.',
  portrait=dict(src='faure-sargent-1889.webp', alt='Oil painting of Gabriel Fauré with wavy grey hair and a large moustache, looking upward',
    cap='<strong>Gabriel Fauré by John Singer Sargent, about 1889</strong>Painted in the same years as the Pavane and the Requiem. Sargent, a friend and admirer, caught Fauré’s dreamy, faraway look. John Singer Sargent, public domain, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Singer_Sargent_-_Gabriel_Faur%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Gabriel Fauré (1845–1924) bridged the Romantic era and modern French music. His harmonies drift through old church modes in ways no one had heard before, and he trained the next generation, including Maurice Ravel.',
  bio=[
    'Fauré was born in Pamiers, in the south of France, on 12 May 1845. As a small boy he played the harmonium in the village chapel, and at nine he was sent to Paris to the École Niedermeyer, a school for church musicians. There he studied the old church modes and Gregorian chant. His piano teacher, Camille Saint-Saëns, introduced him to the music of Liszt and Wagner and became his lifelong friend.',
    'For decades Fauré earned his living as a church organist and choirmaster, mostly at the Église de la Madeleine in Paris, and composed in his summer holidays. He became the great French master of the <em>mélodie</em> (art song), with songs such as “Après un rêve” and “Clair de lune”.',
    'In 1887 he wrote the Pavane, a slow dance for orchestra, inspired by the stately court dances of the 1500s, and dedicated it to the Countess Greffulhe, a leading Paris patron. He added an optional choir singing playful verses by Robert de Montesquiou. In the same years he wrote his gentle Requiem.',
    'In 1896 he became professor of composition at the Paris Conservatoire, where his pupils included Maurice Ravel, Nadia Boulanger and George Enescu, and from 1905 to 1920 he was the Conservatoire’s director. In his last years he lost much of his hearing, and high and low notes sounded distorted to him, yet he kept composing chamber music of great power. He died in Paris on 4 November 1924.'],
  timeline=[('1845','Born in Pamiers, France, on 12 May.'),('1854','Enters the École Niedermeyer in Paris.'),('1861','Begins piano studies with Camille Saint-Saëns.'),('1877','Becomes choirmaster at the Église de la Madeleine.'),('1887','Composes the Pavane, Op. 50, and begins the Requiem.'),('1896','Professor of composition at the Paris Conservatoire; Ravel joins his class in 1897.'),('1905','Becomes director of the Conservatoire.'),('1920','Retires as director as his hearing fails.'),('1924','Dies in Paris on 4 November.')],
  idea_text=['A <strong>pavane</strong> is a slow, dignified procession dance from the 1500s. Dancers stepped in pairs to a steady duple pulse. Fauré keeps that steady walk in the bass, almost like footsteps.',
    'Fauré learned the old <strong>church modes</strong> as a boy. Above the steady pulse he lets harmonies slide unexpectedly, often lowering the leading tone so a cadence sounds soft rather than final. Listen for chords that seem to arrive somewhere unexpected and then calmly carry on.'],
  lab_q='Version A ends a tune with the usual leading tone rising to the home note. Version B uses the lowered seventh of an old mode instead. Which ending sounds older or softer?',
  listen=['Pavane, Op. 50: follow the melody as it passes between instruments over the steady pizzicato (plucked) bass.','Requiem, “Pie Jesu”: hear Fauré’s gentle, flowing harmony set for a solo voice.','“Après un rêve”: listen to a song melody that seems to float above its chords.'],
  practice=['Tap the steady pulse of the bass while the score plays. Does it ever stop?','Find a chord in the score that surprises you. Describe what you expected to hear instead.','Compare Fauré’s Pavane with Ravel’s Pavane for a Dead Princess. What do the two pupil-and-teacher pieces share?'],
  check='The bass keeps a steady walking pulse like the steps of a procession, while the harmony above moves through unexpected, mode-colored chords.',
  related='<a href="ravel.html">Continue to his pupil, Maurice Ravel →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Gabriel-Faure','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Gabriel Fauré'),
)

C['elgar'] = dict(
  name='Edward Elgar', dates='1857–1934', era='Romantic', icon='🎺',
  initial='elgar-nimrod',
  idea='Theme and variations as portraits',
  kicker='Theme and variations as musical portraits',
  desc='Meet Sir Edward Elgar, the self-taught composer who became England’s national voice, and play “Nimrod” from the Enigma Variations and Salut d’Amour in interactive scores.',
  intro='Choose “Nimrod” or Salut d’Amour, then press Play. “Nimrod” grows from a hushed opening to a huge climax and back; Salut d’Amour is a short, tender love song without words. Compare how each one shapes its melody.',
  portrait=dict(src='elgar-lambert-1931.webp', alt='Photograph of an elderly Edward Elgar with white moustache, seated at a desk with papers and a magnifying glass',
    cap='<strong>Sir Edward Elgar, 1931</strong>Elgar at 74, by then Master of the King’s Music and a pioneer of recording his own works on gramophone records. Herbert Lambert, public domain, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Elgar,_posing_for_the_camera_(1931).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Edward Elgar (1857–1934) had almost no formal training and spent years as a provincial violin teacher before, in his forties, he became England’s most celebrated composer. His Enigma Variations paint his friends in music.',
  bio=[
    'Elgar was born on 2 June 1857 in Lower Broadheath, near Worcester, England. His father tuned pianos and ran a music shop, where the boy read every score he could find. He taught himself composition from books and scores and earned a living as a violinist, organist and teacher, and as bandmaster at a local asylum.',
    'In 1888 he gave a short piece for violin and piano to Caroline Alice Roberts as an engagement present. He called it <em>Liebesgruss</em> (“Love’s Greeting”); the publisher renamed it <em>Salut d’Amour</em> to sell more copies, bought it outright for a small fee, and earned a fortune from it. Elgar and Alice married in 1889, and she believed in his gift through years of struggle.',
    'His breakthrough came in 1899 with the <em>Variations on an Original Theme</em>, the “Enigma” Variations. Each of the fourteen variations is a portrait of one of his friends, identified only by initials or nicknames. Variation IX, “Nimrod”, portrays August Jaeger, his editor at the publisher Novello. Jaeger is German for “hunter”, and Nimrod is a mighty hunter in the Bible. The music recalls a conversation in which Jaeger encouraged a discouraged Elgar by talking about Beethoven’s slow movements.',
    'Fame followed: the oratorio <em>The Dream of Gerontius</em> (1900), the <em>Pomp and Circumstance</em> marches (from 1901; the trio of No. 1 is played at graduations across the United States), two symphonies and the Cello Concerto (1919). He was knighted in 1904, became Master of the King’s Music in 1924, and conducted many of his works for early gramophone recordings. He died in Worcester on 23 February 1934.'],
  timeline=[('1857','Born in Lower Broadheath, near Worcester, on 2 June.'),('1879','Becomes bandmaster at the Worcester county asylum at Powick.'),('1888','Writes <em>Salut d’Amour</em> as an engagement gift.'),('1889','Marries Caroline Alice Roberts.'),('1899','The Enigma Variations premiere in London.'),('1900','<em>The Dream of Gerontius</em> premieres in Birmingham.'),('1901','Composes <em>Pomp and Circumstance</em> March No. 1.'),('1904','Knighted.'),('1919','Completes the Cello Concerto.'),('1924','Becomes Master of the King’s Music.'),('1934','Dies in Worcester on 23 February.')],
  idea_text=['In a <strong>theme and variations</strong>, a composer states a melody and then changes it again and again: its speed, rhythm, key, instruments or mood. Elgar made each variation a character sketch of a friend.',
    '“Nimrod” is slow and noble. It begins very quietly, adds instruments and register a little at a time, and reaches a huge climax before fading away. That long, controlled <strong>crescendo</strong> is why it is often played at memorials and ceremonies.'],
  lab_q='Version A plays a tune quickly. Version B plays the same notes slowly with a low bass, the way a variation can transform a theme. Does the character of the tune change?',
  listen=['“Nimrod”: follow the long crescendo. Where is the loudest moment, and how long does the music take to get there?','Enigma Variations, Variation XI (“G.R.S.”): a portrait of an organist’s bulldog falling into a river. Can you hear the splash?','<em>Salut d’Amour</em>: listen to a tender melody that sounds like a singer, though it has no words.'],
  practice=['Find where “Nimrod” is quietest and where it is loudest. How many bars does the build-up take?','Compare the opening bars of <em>Salut d’Amour</em> and “Nimrod”. Which moves faster, and how can you tell from the score?','Choose a friend or family member. Describe the variation you would write to portray them: tempo, loudness, instruments and rhythm.'],
  check='“Nimrod” grows gradually from very quiet to very loud by adding instruments, raising the register and thickening the chords, then falls away again.',
  related='<a href="mascagni.html">Compare an Italian contemporary, Pietro Mascagni →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Edward-Elgar','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Edward Elgar'),
)

C['mascagni'] = dict(
  name='Pietro Mascagni', dates='1863–1945', era='Romantic', icon='🎭',
  initial='mascagni-intermezzo',
  idea='Verismo opera and the orchestral intermezzo',
  kicker='Verismo opera and the intermezzo',
  desc='Meet Pietro Mascagni, whose first opera made him famous overnight, and play the Intermezzo from Cavalleria rusticana in an interactive score.',
  intro='Choose the Intermezzo, then press Play. In the opera, this calm, hymn-like music is heard while the stage is empty, just before the story turns violent. Listen for the long melody that rises to a passionate climax.',
  portrait=dict(src='mascagni-1903.webp', alt='Photograph of a young Pietro Mascagni with thick dark hair, wearing a bow tie',
    cap='<strong>Pietro Mascagni, 1903</strong>Photographed during his American tour, thirteen years after <em>Cavalleria rusticana</em> made him the most talked-about composer in Europe. Library of Congress, public domain, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Mascagni,_1863-1945_LCCN2004681875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Pietro Mascagni (1863–1945) was an unknown small-town music teacher when, at 26, he won a competition with a one-act opera. <em>Cavalleria rusticana</em> launched a new style, verismo, and its Intermezzo became one of the most-played pieces in the world.',
  bio=[
    'Mascagni was born in Livorno, Italy, on 7 December 1863, the son of a baker who wanted him to become a lawyer. He studied music in secret, then at the Milan Conservatory, where he shared lodgings with another poor young composer, Giacomo Puccini. Restless, he left without finishing and toured as a conductor of travelling operetta companies.',
    'He settled in the small town of Cerignola, in southern Italy, as a music teacher. In 1888 the publisher Sonzogno announced a competition for one-act operas. Mascagni’s wife reportedly sent in his score after he had given up on it. <em>Cavalleria rusticana</em> (“Rustic Chivalry”) won, and its premiere in Rome on 17 May 1890 was a sensation, with dozens of curtain calls.',
    'The opera belongs to <strong>verismo</strong> (“realism”): stories about ordinary people, here Sicilian villagers on Easter morning, with jealousy, honor and a fatal duel. The Intermezzo is played between the two scenes, while the stage is empty, a moment of peace before the tragedy.',
    'Mascagni never repeated that first success, though <em>L’amico Fritz</em> (1891) and <em>Iris</em> (1898) are still performed. He became a celebrated conductor. In his last years he was closely associated with Mussolini’s Fascist government, which damaged his reputation; he died in Rome on 2 August 1945, shortly after the end of the Second World War.'],
  timeline=[('1863','Born in Livorno, Italy, on 7 December.'),('1882','Enters the Milan Conservatory; shares lodgings with Giacomo Puccini.'),('1885','Leaves to tour as an operetta conductor.'),('1888','Enters the Sonzogno one-act opera competition.'),('1890','<em>Cavalleria rusticana</em> premieres in Rome on 17 May.'),('1891','<em>L’amico Fritz</em> premieres.'),('1898','<em>Iris</em> premieres.'),('1903','Tours the United States as a conductor.'),('1945','Dies in Rome on 2 August.')],
  idea_text=['<strong>Verismo</strong> (Italian for “realism”) was an opera style of the 1890s about everyday people and their strong emotions, rather than kings and gods. The music is direct and passionate, with big singable melodies.',
    'An <strong>intermezzo</strong> is an orchestral interlude between scenes. Mascagni’s begins like a church hymn, quietly, because it is Easter morning in the village. Then the strings take up a long, soaring melody that tells us something terrible is about to happen.'],
  lab_q='Version A plays a melody softly in one octave. Version B doubles it in octaves, the way strings make a melody soar. Which version sounds more passionate?',
  listen=['Intermezzo from <em>Cavalleria rusticana</em>: compare the hymn-like opening with the soaring melody that follows.','<em>Cavalleria rusticana</em>, “Easter Hymn”: hear the chorus and soprano sing in the village square.','Puccini, <em>La bohème</em>: compare verismo-era music by Mascagni’s old roommate.'],
  practice=['Find where the hymn-like opening ends and the long melody begins. What changes in the score?','Find the highest note of the melody. How does the music lead up to it?','Think of a film scene where nothing is said but something is about to happen. What kind of music would you write for it?'],
  check='The Intermezzo opens quietly in a chorale (hymn) texture, then a long melody rises to a passionate climax before the quiet ending. It gives the audience a calm moment before the tragedy.',
  related='<a href="massenet.html">Compare the French opera master, Jules Massenet →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Pietro-Mascagni','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Pietro Mascagni'),
)

C['barber'] = dict(
  name='Samuel Barber', dates='1910–1981', era='Modern', icon='🎗️',
  initial='barber-classroom-study',
  idea='Long-line melody and suspension',
  kicker='Long-line melody and suspensions',
  desc='Meet Samuel Barber, the American composer of the Adagio for Strings, and explore how long stepwise melodies and suspensions create intense emotion.',
  intro='Barber’s Adagio for Strings (1936) is still under copyright, so it cannot be reproduced here. This workspace holds an original classroom study instead, written to show the technique the Adagio is famous for: a slow melody moving by step over held notes that clash and then resolve. Press Play, then compare it with a recording of the Adagio.',
  portrait=dict(src='barber-van-vechten-1944.webp', alt='Black-and-white photograph of Samuel Barber in dark clothing, seated before a patterned wall',
    cap='<strong>Samuel Barber, 1944</strong>Photographed in New York by Carl Van Vechten, six years after Arturo Toscanini made the Adagio for Strings famous on national radio. Carl Van Vechten, Library of Congress, public domain, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Samuel_Barber_LCCN2004662567.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>.'),
  lede='Samuel Barber (1910–1981) wrote some of the most expressive American music of the twentieth century. He stayed loyal to lyrical melody when many of his colleagues did not, and his Adagio for Strings became the music the United States turns to in moments of grief.',
  bio=[
    'Barber was born in West Chester, Pennsylvania, on 9 March 1910. His aunt was the opera singer Louise Homer. At nine he left a note for his mother saying he was meant to be a composer and asked her not to make him play football. At fourteen he entered the new Curtis Institute of Music in Philadelphia, where he studied composition, piano and singing, and met the Italian composer Gian Carlo Menotti, his lifelong partner.',
    'In 1936, while living in Europe, he wrote a String Quartet. Its slow second movement, arranged for string orchestra as the Adagio for Strings, was premiered on 5 November 1938 by Arturo Toscanini and the NBC Symphony Orchestra in a national radio broadcast. It made him famous. The Adagio was broadcast after the deaths of Franklin D. Roosevelt and John F. Kennedy, and has been played at memorials ever since. Barber later arranged it for choir as <em>Agnus Dei</em> (1967).',
    'His other works include <em>Knoxville: Summer of 1915</em> (1947) for soprano and orchestra, concertos for violin and cello, and the opera <em>Vanessa</em> (1958), which won the Pulitzer Prize. His Piano Concerto won a second Pulitzer in 1963.',
    'In 1966 his opera <em>Antony and Cleopatra</em> opened the new Metropolitan Opera house at Lincoln Center. A lavish production overwhelmed it, the critics were harsh, and Barber wrote little afterward. He died in New York on 23 January 1981.'],
  timeline=[('1910','Born in West Chester, Pennsylvania, on 9 March.'),('1924','Enters the Curtis Institute of Music at fourteen.'),('1936','Composes the String Quartet, Op. 11, with its slow movement.'),('1938','Toscanini premieres the Adagio for Strings on NBC radio.'),('1947','Composes <em>Knoxville: Summer of 1915</em>.'),('1958','Pulitzer Prize for the opera <em>Vanessa</em>.'),('1963','Second Pulitzer Prize, for the Piano Concerto.'),('1966','<em>Antony and Cleopatra</em> opens the new Metropolitan Opera house.'),('1981','Dies in New York on 23 January.')],
  idea_text=['The Adagio for Strings is built from a <strong>long melody that moves almost entirely by step</strong>, one note to the next, never jumping far. Because it has no sudden leaps, it feels like a single breath stretched out.',
    'Barber keeps tension high with <strong>suspensions</strong>: one voice holds a note while the harmony changes beneath it, creating a clash (dissonance) that then resolves down by step. Chains of suspensions, climbing slowly higher, build to the famous climax and its sudden silence.'],
  lab_q='Version A plays a stepwise melody note by note. Version B lets each note overlap the next, so they clash before resolving. Which sounds more intense?',
  listen=['Adagio for Strings: follow the opening melody and count how many notes move by step versus by leap.','Listen for the climax, where the violins play very high and loud, and then for the silence that follows.','<em>Agnus Dei</em>: compare Barber’s choral version of the same music. What changes when voices sing it?'],
  practice=['Play the classroom study and point to each suspension: where does a held note clash, and where does it resolve?','Hum the opening of the Adagio from a recording. Does it ever leap? Describe the shape of its line.','Why might composers be cautious about quoting copyrighted music in a public lesson? What are other ways to study it?'],
  check='A suspension holds a note over a changing chord so it clashes, then resolves down a step. Chains of them, rising in pitch, are what build the intensity of the Adagio.',
  related='<a href="gershwin.html">Compare another American voice, George Gershwin →</a>',
  link=('https://www.britannica.com/biography/Samuel-Barber','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Samuel Barber'),
)
